! An iodine atom in a box: a magnetic insulator with spin-orbit coupling.
!
! The smallest cell that carries an orbital magnetization at all. A neutral
! iodine atom is 5s2 5p5, one hole in the p shell, so Hund's rules give S = 1/2,
! L = 1 and -- the shell being more than half full -- J = L + S = 3/2 with the
! orbital moment parallel to the spin. Spin-orbit coupling is what locks them
! together, and a fully relativistic norm-conserving dataset is what carries it.
!
! `occupations = 'fixed'` with nbnd = 8: seven spinor bands hold the seven
! valence electrons and the eighth is empty, 0.16 eV above. That gap is what
! makes the manifold isolated, which every Berry-phase quantity needs. A
! smearing wide enough to converge this atom comfortably (degauss = 0.005 Ry)
! puts a fifth of an electron in the eighth band and the manifold stops being
! one; fixed occupations converge in 9 iterations here and need no such choice.
!
! 12 bohr of box leaves the periodic images overlapping slightly, which is
! deliberate: the bands acquire a little dispersion, and a k-space Berry phase
! of a set of flat bands is not a test of anything.
 &control
    calculation = 'scf'
 /
 &system
    ibrav = 1, celldm(1) = 12.0, nat = 1, ntyp = 1, ecutwfc = 45.0,
    occupations = 'fixed', nbnd = 8, nosym = .true., noinv = .true.,
    noncolin = .true., lspinorb = .true.,
    starting_magnetization(1) = 0.5, angle1(1) = 0.0
 /
 &electrons
    conv_thr = 1.0d-10, mixing_beta = 0.3
 /
ATOMIC_SPECIES
 I 126.9 I.rel-pbe-nc-dojo.UPF
ATOMIC_POSITIONS crystal
 I 0.00 0.00 0.00
K_POINTS (automatic)
 1 1 1 0 0 0
